title: "Follow a mailbox from any HTTP client"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Follow a mailbox from any HTTP client}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("tools/guides/setup.R")
```

Pi sessions and harness lanes wake on mail through an NNG subscription. A
client without an NNG binding, such as a browser `EventSource`, a dashboard,
or `curl`, can follow the same wake-ups as Server-Sent Events. The endpoint
serves them when `--sse` names an HTTP listener.

```{bash}
node tools/pi-coordination-endpoint.ts "$STATE/coordination.url" "$STATE/coordination.duckdb" \
  --sse http://127.0.0.1:0 > "$STATE/endpoint.log" 2>&1 &
echo $! > "$STATE/endpoint.pid"
until [ -s "$STATE/coordination.url" ]; do sleep 0.1; done
```

## A stream per mailbox

`register` returns the mailbox's stream URL. Its `topic` is the same opaque
value the NNG hints use, and it is the capability: without a registration it
cannot be derived.

```{r}
url <- guide_url()
reader <- register("reader")
writer <- register("writer")
sse <- reader$events$sse_url
writeLines(sse, file.path(guide_state, "sse.url"))
sub("^http://127\\.0\\.0\\.1:[0-9]+", "http://127.0.0.1:<port>", sse)
```

`curl -N` follows the stream in the background, and `writer` sends two
messages to `reader`:

```{bash}
curl -sN --max-time 4 "$(cat "$STATE/sse.url")" > "$STATE/sse.txt" 2> /dev/null &
echo $! > "$STATE/curl.pid"
until grep -q ': ready' "$STATE/sse.txt" 2> /dev/null; do sleep 0.05; done
```

```{r}
for (key in c("first", "second")) {
  invisible(rpc_call(url, "send", list(
    registration_id = writer$registration_id, recipient_agent_id = "reader",
    idempotency_key = key, content = paste("mail", key)
  )))
}
```

The stream carried one `mail` event per message, each naming the topic and
nothing else:

```{bash}
tail --pid="$(cat "$STATE/curl.pid")" -f /dev/null
cat "$STATE/sse.txt"
```

A wake-up is a prompt to call `receive`, which is where the mail is:

```{r}
inbox <- rpc_call(url, "receive", list(registration_id = reader$registration_id, limit = 8))
vapply(inbox$messages, `[[`, "", "content")
```

## What the listener refuses

A topic that names no registered mailbox gets no stream. The listener is an
HTTP service, and every ducknng HTTP service also mounts framed RPC, so its
authorizer refuses everything but the event route. The coordination methods
stay behind the endpoint's own `ipc://` URL:

```{bash}
base="$(sed 's/?.*//' "$STATE/sse.url")"
printf 'unknown topic: %s\n' "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$base?topic=00000000000000000000000000000000")"
printf 'no topic: %s\n' "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$base")"
# A manifest request: version 1, type 0, then 20 zero bytes of header.
{ printf '\001'; head -c 21 /dev/zero; } > "$STATE/manifest.frame"
printf 'framed RPC: %s\n' "$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' -X POST \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/vnd.ducknng.frame' \
  --data-binary @"$STATE/manifest.frame" "${base%/events}/_ducknng")"
```

With mutual TLS the listener must be `https://` and asks for the same client
certificates as the endpoint. The relay behind each stream holds no DuckDB
connection, so open streams never slow the SQL methods, and stopping the
endpoint ends them.

```{bash}
#| include: false
kill "$(cat "$STATE/endpoint.pid")"
```

```{r, include = FALSE}
events <- readLines(file.path(guide_state, "sse.txt"))
topic <- reader$events$topic
stopifnot(
  identical(events[[1]], ": ready"),
  sum(events == "event: mail") == 2L,
  sum(events == paste("data:", topic)) == 2L,
  length(inbox$messages) == 2L
)
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
cat("> GUIDE_SSE_VERIFIED: curl followed one mailbox and saw one mail event per message\n")
```
